<v NARRATOR>From Spark and Anvil and DialogueQuest — this is Aim, in chapter 1 of the DialogueQuest cast.

2
00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:15.261
<v NARRATOR>This drama uses an AI-generated voice. Voiced via Gemini 2.5.

3
00:00:15.261 --> 00:00:23.512
<v NARRATOR>Aim is a keen-eyed kestrel, still and watchful on a fence post in the sharp evening air.

4
00:00:23.512 --> 00:00:33.523
<v NARRATOR>When she speaks, her speech bubble is not round. It is pointed — shaped like a little arrow — and it points at something.

5
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:47.654
<v AIM>It's getting cold out here.

6
00:00:47.654 --> 00:00:56.345
<v NARRATOR>But her arrow does not point at the sky or the weather. It points straight at the cottage door.

7
00:00:56.345 --> 00:01:15.196
<v AIM>My name is Aim. I keep the purpose — what a line is trying to *do*. I didn't really want to talk about the cold. I wanted to go inside. So that is where my words were aimed.

8
00:01:15.196 --> 00:01:21.287
<v NARRATOR>Patter the writing coach watched her arrows swing from target to target.

9
00:01:21.287 --> 00:01:40.618
<v AIM>"How was your test?" — aimed at *I'm worried about you.* "Nice jacket." — aimed at *I want you to like me.* "I'm not hungry." — aimed at *leave me alone.*

10
00:01:40.618 --> 00:01:56.669
<v AIM>People don't talk just to share facts. They talk to *get* something — to be reassured, to dodge, to win, to be loved, to push someone away.

11
00:01:56.669 --> 00:02:09.520
<v NARRATOR>Then she showed him the dead kind. She made a character say a flat line, all facts and no want, and its bubble hung there round and aimless.

12
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:24.611
<v AIM>That line isn't aimed at anything. It's just information. Nobody talks like that. It's dead on the page.

13
00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:35.382
<v NARRATOR>Patter felt the click — his young writers' characters explained things at each other, but nobody was ever trying to *get* anything.

14
00:02:35.382 --> 00:02:56.113
<v AIM>Before you write a line, ask: what is this character trying to *do* with it? Not just say — *do*. Are they asking? Dodging? Bragging? Apologizing without apologizing?

15
00:02:56.113 --> 00:03:04.684
<v NARRATOR>She wrote three words on the board — *You're home late* — and the young writers found four different arrows underneath them.

16
00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:30.895
<v AIM>Same three words. Four different wants. The aim is what makes the line *mean* something. A line with no aim is a line with no life.

17
00:03:30.895 --> 00:03:42.026
<v NARRATOR>And in the dusk Aim folded her wings, content — for seeing the want behind the words, she said, is not cold at all.

18
00:03:42.026 --> 00:04:03.277
<v AIM>When I notice that "it's cold in here" really means *please sit closer*, I understand someone truly. Seeing the arrow is the closest kind of listening there is.

19
